base.decided:
The Committee decided to proceed with the next phase of research, and the results were more promising. It is not yet possible for us to offer a detailed account of the study in this book, but we shall briefly outline its main conclusions here:

i)     The existence of other species of humans outside that group which is most commonly referred to as ‘human’ should be treated seriously, but there are limits to our ability to identify them;
ii)    There was probably another hominid on the planet long before modern man appeared;
iii)  Homo sapiens, including Neanderthals, have been present throughout history;
iv)  Early modern human populations lived at the same time (and place) as some of these other human types and shared much of their environment and culture;
v)   The survival of modern humans today must ultimately depend upon our efforts to preserve biodiversity rather than extinguish it;
vi)  The extinction of one or more hominids may well be followed

base.participants:
In their discussion of the economic situation and the outlook, meeting participants agreed that:

  • The U.S. is in a recession but not yet on a full-fledged depression;
  • There was hope for growth over 2 percent this year with improved confidence from consumers;
  • Confidence had been high among firms since 2014 but expectations were more realistic now because of concerns about supply and price levels;
  • As the economy tightened further during Q3, there would be an increase in household spending as families prepared to pay off debt or lower mortgage payments;
  • More government actions by Congress could help stimulate private demand, like tax cuts and infrastructure investment projects;
  • A better recovery might occur sooner than anticipated given the low level of unemployment, which will bring wages back into line and allow businesses to start hiring again.
